\section{A Grammar for the IR, Not the Source Program}

The Graham--Glanville approach starts with an intermediate representation that is already a tree and turns it into a token stream for a shift--reduce parser. In ``Expressing Instructions as a Grammar'' and ``Tree Parsing,'' Blindell describes the key move: print the IR's ordered operators and leaves in prefix notation, then let reductions stand for instruction selections \cite[section~3.3.1]{blindell2016instruction}. The grammar now describes the target's ways of implementing the IR. Its parse trees are instruction derivations, not alternative interpretations of the original program.

An operator's fixed arity is enough to delimit its child expressions in the prefix stream. A nullary token can carry a payload that does not appear in the grammar: $\mathrm{ARG}(a)$ and $\mathrm{ARG}(b)$ have the same terminal class but name different incoming virtual registers. A rule consists of a production, a cost, and an action. We write $G\gets p$ to mean that the pattern $p$ can be reduced to the goal $G$. A successful parse consumes the entire stream and produces the requested root goal. Reducing a prefix to some nonterminal is not acceptance.

\section{Two Parses for a Byte Pack}

The IR operation $\mathrm{PACK}$ returns a word with the low byte of its first operand in bits 8 through 15, the low byte of its second operand in bits 0 through 7, and all other bits zero. $\mathrm{MASK}$ zeros all but the low byte. The machine's generic pack instruction requires its low operand to have already been masked; its immediate-high form does the low masking itself. The grammar below covers packs whose second operand has an explicit mask. $R$ means a register, $L$ a register known to hold only an unsigned byte, and $G$ the final result in a register. $\mathrm{K}$ denotes a literal already classified as an unsigned byte. We expose the fused instruction only when the low operand is an incoming register.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{clrl}
Rule & Prefix production & Cost & Action \\
$r_1$ & $R\gets \mathrm{ARG}$ & 0 & Reuse the incoming register \\
$r_2$ & $R\gets \mathrm{K}$ & 1 & Materialize the literal \\
$r_3$ & $L\gets \mathrm{MASK}\ R$ & 1 & Clear the upper bits \\
$r_4$ & $G\gets \mathrm{PACK}\ R\ L$ & 1 & Pack from two registers \\
$r_5$ & $G\gets \mathrm{PACK}\ \mathrm{K}\ \mathrm{MASK}\ \mathrm{ARG}$ & 1
      & Pack with immediate high byte \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

For $\mathrm{PACK}(\mathrm{K}(18),\mathrm{MASK}(\mathrm{ARG}(a)))$, the fused parse consumes all four raw tokens in one reduction. The other parse reduces the high literal to $R$, the low argument to $R$, the mask to $L$, and the pack to $G$. Both are legal, with modeled costs one and three. In the fused parse the action knows the high byte and the incoming register from terminal payloads; in the generic parse it consumes the operands returned by its frontier reductions. This is ambiguity in how to implement a computation whose semantics are fixed.

