\section{Follow the Lookahead into a Dead End}

Replace $\mathrm{ARG}(a)$ with $\mathrm{K}(52)$. The complete input is now \texttt{PACK K(18) MASK K(52)}. The table shows the stack's symbols, omitting parser states and semantic payloads. The two branches start with exactly the same stack and lookahead.

\begin{verbatim}
action                       stack                 next
shift PACK, shift K(18)      PACK K                MASK
[1] try shifting MASK        PACK K MASK           K(52)
    no valid continuation    --                    --

resume before [1]            PACK K                MASK
[2] reduce r2                PACK R                MASK
shift MASK, shift K(52)      PACK R MASK K         end
reduce r2, then r3           PACK R L              end
reduce r4                    G                     end
accept                       G                     end
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{[1]} Shifting postpones commitment to the register form and keeps the longer rule available. But after that shift the longer rule needs $\mathrm{ARG}$, not $\mathrm{K}$. The first literal is now buried under $\mathrm{MASK}$, where an ordinary stack reduction cannot materialize it. Blindell calls this failure to find an existing derivation \emph{syntactic blocking}.

\noindent\textbf{[2]} Reducing the first literal while it is on top of the stack rules out the fused parse but keeps the generic one viable. The four emitted operations materialize 18 and 52, mask the second value, and pack the bytes to give \texttt{0x1234}. Resuming here illustrates a second branch of the derivation, not a recovery feature of an ordinary deterministic LR parser.

\section{Acceptance, Legality, and Cost}

\Cref{fig:visual-265} connects the dead end to the exact lookahead that caused it. The alternative changes the parse stack, not the input.

