After a reduction, the new stack symbol must summarize exactly the consecutive input segment consumed by that production, and its payload must satisfy the nonterminal's operand contract. In particular, a register named by $L$ must be the result of an action that clears the upper bits. An implementation that tries multiple branches can build deferred action records and run them in dependency order only after accepting, so failed parses cannot leave partially emitted code behind.

Blindell's ``Resolving Conflicts and Avoiding Blocking'' distinguishes this syntactic failure from \emph{semantic blocking}: several reductions can look valid symbolically but their operand predicates may all fail. ``Extending Grammars with Semantic Handling'' discusses carrying attributes and testing predicates before running actions \cite[sections~3.3.1--3.3.2]{blindell2016instruction}. For instance, a tighter immediate encoding could use a narrower terminal class or a range test, retaining register materialization for values outside the range. Changing how conflicts are resolved may fix one input and hurt another: always reducing at the conflict above would lose the cheaper fused parse on the original input. The LR table tells a parser which reduction to try; it does not, by itself, compare complete derivations. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-266} takes a different approach to finding candidates without committing to their reductions.
